.br
\fBvector destroy\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
\fBvector expr\fP \fIexpression\fP
.br
\fBvector names\fP \fIpattern\fP
.br
.SH ::RBC
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBbarchart\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a barchart widget\&. A barchart uses bar elements as its default element type\&. It uses the same graph
widget infrastructure and common components as graph, but elements created through the generic
GRAPHINST element interface are bar elements by default\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget
is created are the same options accepted later by GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBbarchart\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the barchart widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
barchart \&.b -title "Example barchart" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.b -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created barchart widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBgraph\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a graph widget\&. A graph uses line elements as its default element type\&. The widget supports the
common graph components such as axes, elements, markers, pens, legends, grids, crosshairs, and PostScript
output\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget is created are the same options accepted later by
GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBgraph\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the graph widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g -title "Example graph" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.g -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created graph widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the graph's virtual axes\&. Virtual axes can be created, deleted, configured, transformed, bound to events, and mapped to graph margins\&.
.TP
\fIbar\fP
Performs an element operation using bar elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the graph widget\&.
.TP
\fIcget\fP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&. Current value of option\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP
Queries or modifies graph widget configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns configuration information for all widget options\&. With one option, returns configuration information for that option\&. With one or more option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&.
.TP
\fIcrosshairs\fP
Performs an operation on the graph crosshairs\&.
.TP
\fIelement\fP
Performs an operation on graph elements using the widget's default element type\&. The default is a line element for graph, a bar element for barchart, and a strip element for stripchart\&.
.TP
\fIextents\fP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&. For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.TP
\fIgrid\fP
Performs an operation on the graph grid\&.
.TP
\fIinside\fP
Tests whether a window coordinate lies inside the graph plotting area\&.
.TP
\fIinvtransform\fP
Converts a point from window coordinates to graph coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&. winX and winY may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIlegend\fP
Performs an operation on the graph legend\&.
.TP
\fIline\fP
Performs an element operation using line elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the graph widget\&.
.TP
\fImarker\fP
Performs an operation on graph markers\&.
.TP
\fIpen\fP
Performs an operation on graph pens\&. Pens define reusable drawing attributes for line and bar elements\&.
.TP
\fIpostscript\fP
Performs a PostScript output operation for the graph\&.
.TP
\fIsnap\fP
Renders a snapshot of the graph\&. By default the snapshot is written to an existing Tk photo image\&. On Windows, snapshots can also be written as Windows Metafile (wmf) or Enhanced Metafile (emf) output\&.
.TP
\fItransform\fP
Converts a point from graph coordinates to window coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&. x and y may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIx2axis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the top margin\&.
.TP
\fIxaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the bottom margin\&.
.TP
\fIy2axis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the right margin\&.
.TP
\fIyaxis\fP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this is the left margin\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph's virtual axes\&. Virtual axes can be created, deleted, configured,
transformed, bound to events, and mapped to graph margins\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST bar\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an element operation using bar elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the
graph widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST bar\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST cget\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&.
Current value of option\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST cget\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fIWidget options\fP for the complete list of supported options and their descriptions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current value of a graph widget configuration option\&.
Current value of option\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or modifies graph widget configuration options\&. With no arguments, returns configuration information
for all widget options\&. With one option, returns configuration information for that option\&. With one or more
option/value pairs, changes the specified options\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fP \fIoption\fP
.br
\fBGRAPHINST configure\fP \fI?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
New value for the option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
See \fIWidget options\fP for the complete list of supported options and their descriptions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Configuration information when querying; nothing when setting options\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST crosshairs\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph crosshairs\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST crosshairs\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected crosshairs operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST element\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph elements using the widget's default element type\&. The default is a line
element for graph, a bar element for barchart, and a strip element for stripchart\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST element\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST extents\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&.
For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and
legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST extents\fP \fIitem\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
item
Component whose extents are requested\&. Must be plotheight, plotwidth, plotarea, legend, leftmargin, rightmargin, topmargin, or bottommargin\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the size or geometry of a graph component\&.
For plotheight, plotwidth, and the margin items, returns one size in pixels\&. For plotarea and
legend, returns a list of four values in the form x y width height\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST grid\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph grid\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST grid\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected grid operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST inside\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Tests whether a window coordinate lies inside the graph plotting area\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST inside\fP \fIwinX winY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
winX
X coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
winY
Y coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Boolean value indicating whether the point is inside the plotting area\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST invtransform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a point from window coordinates to graph coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&.
winX and winY may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST invtransform\fP \fIwinX winY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
winX
X coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.TP
winY
Y coordinate in window coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A two-element list containing the corresponding graph coordinates x y\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST legend\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the graph legend\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST legend\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected legend operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST line\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an element operation using line elements explicitly, regardless of the default element type of the
graph widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST line\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected element operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST marker\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph markers\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST marker\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected marker operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST pen\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on graph pens\&. Pens define reusable drawing attributes for line and bar elements\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST pen\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected pen operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST postscript\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs a PostScript output operation for the graph\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST postscript\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected PostScript operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST snap\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Renders a snapshot of the graph\&. By default the snapshot is written to an existing Tk photo image\&. On
Windows, snapshots can also be written as Windows Metafile (wmf) or Enhanced Metafile (emf) output\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST snap\fP \fIname ?-format format? ?-height height? ?-width width?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-format format
Output format\&. photo is available on all platforms\&. wmf and emf are available on Windows\&. The default is photo\&.
.TP
-height height
Height of the rendered snapshot\&. If omitted, the current widget height is used\&.
.TP
-width width
Width of the rendered snapshot\&. If omitted, the current widget width is used\&.
.TP
name
For photo format, name of an existing Tk photo image; for wmf or emf, output filename\&. On Windows, CLIPBOARD writes metafile output to the clipboard instead of a file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
image create photo graphImage
\&.g snap graphImage

\&.g snap graphImage -width 1200 -height 800
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST transform\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Converts a point from graph coordinates to window coordinates using the graph's primary X and Y axes\&.
x and y may be Tcl expressions that evaluate to numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST transform\fP \fIx y\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
X coordinate in graph coordinates\&.
.TP
y
Y coordinate in graph coordinates\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A two-element list containing the corresponding integer window coordinates x y\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST x2axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation
this is the top margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST x2axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST xaxis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary X-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this
is the bottom margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST xaxis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST y2axis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the secondary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation
this is the right margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST y2axis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBGRAPHINST yaxis\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Performs an operation on the axis mapped to the primary Y-axis margin\&. With a normal graph orientation this
is the left margin\&.
.RS 4n
\fBGRAPHINST yaxis\fP \fIoperation ?args \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Result of the selected margin-axis operation\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fInatural\fP
Computes a natural cubic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source X range are extrapolated using the cubic polynomial of the first or last spline interval\&.
.TP
\fIquadratic\fP
Computes a shape-preserving quadratic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source X range are extrapolated using the spline defined by the first or last source interval\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline natural\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Computes a natural cubic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at every X
coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the source
X range are extrapolated using the cubic polynomial of the first or last spline interval\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline natural\fP \fIx y splX splY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
Vector containing the finite, strictly increasing X coordinates of the source points\&.
.TP
y
Vector containing the finite Y coordinates of the source points; it must have the same length as x\&.
.TP
splX
Vector containing finite X coordinates at which the spline is evaluated; values may be in any order and may lie outside the source X range\&.
.TP
splY
Vector in which the interpolated or extrapolated Y coordinates are stored; it is created or resized as necessary\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBspline quadratic\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Computes a shape-preserving quadratic spline through the source vectors x and y and evaluates it at
every X coordinate in splX\&. Evaluation coordinates may appear in any order\&. Coordinates outside the
source X range are extrapolated using the spline defined by the first or last source interval\&.
.RS 4n
\fBspline quadratic\fP \fIx y splX splY\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
x
Vector containing the finite, strictly increasing X coordinates of the source points\&.
.TP
y
Vector containing the finite Y coordinates of the source points; it must have the same length as x\&.
.TP
splX
Vector containing finite X coordinates at which the spline is evaluated; values may be in any order and may lie outside the source X range\&.
.TP
splY
Vector in which the interpolated or extrapolated Y coordinates are stored; it is created or resized as necessary\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBstripchart\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a stripchart widget\&. A stripchart uses strip elements as its default element type\&. It uses the same
graph widget infrastructure and common components as graph, but elements created through the generic
GRAPHINST element interface are strip elements by default\&. Configuration options supplied when the widget
is created are the same options accepted later by GRAPHINST configure\&.
.RS 4n
\fBstripchart\fP \fIpathName ?option value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pathName
Tk pathname of the stripchart widget to create\&.
.TP
option
Graph widget configuration option\&.
.TP
value
Value for the configuration option\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
stripchart \&.s -title "Example stripchart" -width 800 -height 500
pack \&.s -fill both -expand yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The pathname of the newly created stripchart widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fI*\fP
Multiplies every value of the vector by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&. list may name another vector or contain a scalar value\&. If another vector is used, it must have the same number of selected values\&.
.TP
\fI+\fP
Adds a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.TP
\fI-\fP
Subtracts a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.TP
\fI/\fP
Divides every vector value by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
.TP
\fIappend\fP
Appends values to the vector\&. Each item may be another vector or a Tcl list of numeric values\&.
.TP
\fIbinread\fP
Reads native binary numeric values from a Tcl channel into the vector\&.
.TP
\fIclear\fP
Clears cached elements of the Tcl array associated with the vector\&. This does not remove values from the vector\&. It can be used to release memory occupied by cached array-index entries\&.
.TP
\fIdelete\fP
Deletes one or more indices or index ranges from the vector\&. Remaining values are compacted after the deleted values are removed\&.
.TP
\fIdup\fP
Copies this vector into another vector\&. The destination vector is created if necessary\&. If it already exists, its contents are replaced\&.
.TP
\fIexpr\fP
Evaluates expression and stores the result in this vector\&.
.TP
\fIindex\fP
Reads or writes a vector value or range\&.  With no value argument, returns the value or values selected by index\&. With value, assigns that numeric value to every element selected by index\&. The special ++end index may be used when setting a value to append one new element\&.
.TP
\fIlength\fP
Queries or changes the vector length\&. If newSize is supplied, the vector is resized\&. New elements created by enlarging the vector are initialized by the vector resizing machinery\&.
.TP
\fImerge\fP
Interleaves values from two or more vectors and stores the result in this vector\&. All input vectors must contain the same number of selected values\&.
.TP
\fInormalize\fP
Normalizes vector values to the range 0\&.0 through 1\&.0 using: (value - minimum) / (maximum - minimum) With no destination vector, returns the normalized values as a Tcl list\&. If vecName is supplied, that vector is created or resized and the normalized values are stored there\&.
.TP
\fIoffset\fP
Queries or changes the external index offset of the vector\&. The offset changes the numeric indices through which the vector is addressed without moving the underlying data\&.
.TP
\fIpopulate\fP
Creates a more densely sampled vector using linear interpolation\&.  density intermediate values are inserted between every adjacent pair of source values\&. density must be at least 1\&. The destination may be the same vector as the source\&.
.TP
\fIrandom\fP
Fills the vector with pseudo-random values in the range # [0\&.0, 1\&.0)\&. The current vector length is preserved\&. Each existing component is replaced by a newly generated pseudo-random value\&. An empty vector remains empty\&.
.TP
\fIrange\fP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This operation does not modify the vector\&. A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.TP
\fIsearch\fP
Searches the vector for values in a numeric range\&. With one numeric argument, returns matches equal to that value\&. With two numeric arguments, returns matches between min and max, inclusive within the implementation's floating-point tolerance\&. By default matching vector indices are returned\&. With -value, matching vector values are returned instead\&.
.TP
\fIseq\fP
Generates an arithmetic sequence\&.
.TP
\fIset\fP
Replaces the contents of the vector\&. list may be a Tcl list of numeric values or the name of another vector\&.
.TP
\fIsort\fP
Sorts this vector\&. Additional vectors may be supplied\&. They must have the same length and are rearranged using the same permutation, allowing associated data to remain aligned\&. -reverse requests descending rather than ascending order\&.
.TP
\fIsplit\fP
Distributes values from this vector among destination vectors in round-robin order\&. The source vector length must be evenly divisible by the number of destination vectors\&. Existing destination contents are preserved and the split values are appended\&.
.TP
\fIvariable\fP
Queries or changes the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. Supplying an empty variable name removes the current mapping\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST *\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Multiplies every value of the vector by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
list may name another vector or contain a scalar value\&. If another vector is used, it must have the same
number of selected values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST *\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself # is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST +\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Adds a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST +\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself # is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST -\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Subtracts a scalar or another vector component-by-component\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST -\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself # is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST /\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Divides every vector value by a scalar or by the corresponding value of another vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST /\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Vector name or scalar value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list containing the resulting values\&. The vector itself # is not modified\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST append\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Appends values to the vector\&. Each item may be another vector or a Tcl list of numeric values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST append\fP \fIitem ?item \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
item
Vector name or list of numeric values\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST binread\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads native binary numeric values from a Tcl channel into the vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST binread\fP \fIchannel ?numValues? ?-at index? ?-format format? ?-swap?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-at index
Starts storing values at index\&. Existing values are # overwritten from that position\&. Without -at, values # are appended to the vector\&.
.TP
-format format
Native numeric input representation\&. Formats have the form i#, u#, r#, or f#, where # is # the native type size in bytes\&. The default is the # native C double representation\&.
.TP
-swap
Reverses the byte order of each input value\&.
.TP
channel
Name of a readable Tcl channel\&.
.TP
numValues
Optional maximum number of values to read\&. A value of zero, or omission of the argument, reads until EOF\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The channel is temporarily placed in binary translation mode and its previous translation setting is
restored before the command returns\&. A read that ends in the middle of a binary value is an error\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Number of complete values read\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST clear\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Clears cached elements of the Tcl array associated with the vector\&. This does not remove values from the
vector\&. It can be used to release memory occupied by cached array-index entries\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST clear\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST delete\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Deletes one or more indices or index ranges from the vector\&. Remaining values are compacted after the
deleted values are removed\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST delete\fP \fIindex ?index \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
index
Vector index or colon-separated index range\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST dup\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Copies this vector into another vector\&. The destination vector is created if necessary\&. If it already
exists, its contents are replaced\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST dup\fP \fIvecName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST expr\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Evaluates expression and stores the result in this vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST expr\fP \fIexpression\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
expression
Vector expression\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST index\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Reads or writes a vector value or range\&.  With no value argument, returns the value or values selected by
index\&. With value, assigns that numeric value to every element selected by index\&. The special
++end index may be used when setting a value to append one new element\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST index\fP \fIindex\fP
.br
\fBVECINST index\fP \fIindex value\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
index
Vector index or range\&.
.TP
value
Optional numeric value\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Selected value(s) when querying, or the assigned value when setting\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST length\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the vector length\&. If newSize is supplied, the vector is resized\&. New elements
created by enlarging the vector are initialized by the vector resizing machinery\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST length\fP \fInewSize\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
newSize
New non-negative vector length\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current vector length\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST merge\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Interleaves values from two or more vectors and stores the result in this vector\&. All input vectors must
contain the same number of selected values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST merge\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Input vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
For vectors a={1 2} and b={10 20}, merging a b produces:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
1 10 2 20
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST normalize\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Normalizes vector values to the range 0\&.0 through 1\&.0 using: (value - minimum) / (maximum - minimum)
With no destination vector, returns the normalized values as a Tcl list\&. If vecName is supplied, that
vector is created or resized and the normalized values are stored there\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST normalize\fP \fIvecName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Optional destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Normalized list when no destination is specified; otherwise # nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST offset\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the external index offset of the vector\&. The offset changes the numeric indices
through which the vector is addressed without moving the underlying data\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST offset\fP \fIoffset\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
offset
New Tcl-sized integer offset\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Current offset\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST populate\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates a more densely sampled vector using linear interpolation\&.  density intermediate values are
inserted between every adjacent pair of source values\&. density must be at least 1\&. The destination may
be the same vector as the source\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST populate\fP \fIvecName density\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.TP
density
Number of interpolated values inserted between each pair\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST random\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Fills the vector with pseudo-random values in the range # [0\&.0, 1\&.0)\&. The current vector length is
preserved\&. Each existing component is replaced by a newly generated pseudo-random value\&. An empty vector
remains empty\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST random\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x length 100
x random
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST range\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are
returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending
index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This
operation does not modify the vector\&.
A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST range\fP \fIfirst last\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
first
First vector index\&.
.TP
last
Last vector index\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x set {10 20 30 40 50}

x range 1 3
# -> 20\&.0 30\&.0 40\&.0

x range 3 1
# -> 40\&.0 30\&.0 20\&.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns an inclusive range of vector values\&. If first is less than or equal to last, values are
returned in ascending index order\&. If first is greater than last, values are returned in descending
index order\&. first and last are normal vector indices and may use forms such as end\&. This
operation does not modify the vector\&.
A list containing the selected vector values\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST search\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Searches the vector for values in a numeric range\&. With one numeric argument, returns matches equal to
that value\&. With two numeric arguments, returns matches between min and max, inclusive within the
implementation's floating-point tolerance\&. By default matching vector indices are returned\&. With -value,
matching vector values are returned instead\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST search\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fIvalue1 value2\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fI-value value\fP
.br
\fBVECINST search\fP \fI-value value1 value2\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-value
Return values instead of indices\&.
.TP
max
Optional upper bound\&.
.TP
min
Lower bound, or the exact value when max is omitted\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
List of matching indices or values\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST seq\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Generates an arithmetic sequence\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST seq\fP \fIstart end ?step?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
end
Numeric end value or literal end\&.
.TP
start
First value\&.
.TP
step
Optional increment\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
With a numeric end, the vector is resized to hold the generated sequence:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x seq 0 10 0\&.5
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the second argument is the literal end, the vector keeps its current length and is filled starting at
start using step:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
x length 100
x seq 0 end 0\&.1
.fi
.RE
.PP
step defaults to 1\&.0 and must be non-zero\&. Sequence bounds and step must be finite\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST set\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Replaces the contents of the vector\&. list may be a Tcl list of numeric values or the name of another
vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST set\fP \fIlist\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
list
Numeric list or vector name\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST sort\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Sorts this vector\&. Additional vectors may be supplied\&. They must have the same length and are
rearranged using the same permutation, allowing associated data to remain aligned\&. -reverse requests
descending rather than ascending order\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST sort\fP \fI?-reverse? ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-reverse
Sort in descending order\&.
.TP
vecName
Optional associated vector to reorder\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST split\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Distributes values from this vector among destination vectors in round-robin order\&. The source vector
length must be evenly divisible by the number of destination vectors\&. Existing destination contents are
preserved and the split values are appended\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST split\fP \fI?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Destination vector\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
For example, splitting:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
{1 10 2 20 3 30}
.fi
.RE
.PP
into vectors x and y appends {1 2 3} to x and {10 20 30} to y\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBVECINST variable\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Queries or changes the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. Supplying an empty variable name
removes the current mapping\&.
.RS 4n
\fBVECINST variable\fP \fIvarName\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
varName
Optional Tcl array variable name\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Name of the currently mapped variable, or an empty result # if no variable is mapped\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
A
command
ensemble\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector\fP \fIsubcommand \&.\&.\&.\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The ensemble supports the following subcommands:
.RS 4n
.TP
\fIcreate\fP
Creates one or more vectors\&.
.TP
\fIdestroy\fP
Destroys one or more vectors\&. Destroying a vector removes its associated vector instance command and Tcl array variable and notifies clients that use the vector\&.
.TP
\fIexpr\fP
Evaluates a vector expression\&. expression may contain scalars, vectors, vector ranges, arithmetic operators, and the vector mathematical functions described in \fIVector expressions\fP\&. Unlike the instance \fI::rbc::VECINST::expr\fP operation, this command does not store the result in an existing vector\&.
.TP
\fInames\fP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob pattern are returned\&. A list of vector names\&.
.RE
.PP
Refer to the documentation of each subcommand for details\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector create\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Creates one or more vectors\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector create\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.? ?switch value \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-command commandName
Sets the Tcl command associated with the vector\&. This option can only be used when creating one vector\&.
.TP
-flush boolean
If true, cached entries in the associated Tcl array are flushed whenever the vector is modified\&.
.TP
-length size
Sets the initial length when no size is included in the vector specification\&. size must be zero or greater\&.
.TP
-variable varName
Sets the Tcl array variable associated with the vector\&. This option can only be used when creating one vector\&.
.TP
-watchunset boolean
If true, unsetting the complete associated # array variable destroys the vector\&.
.TP
vecName
Vector name or vector specification\&. A specification may have the form name, name(size), or name(first:last)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
::rbc::vector create x
::rbc::vector create y(100)
::rbc::vector create z(-10:10)
set v [::rbc::vector create #auto]
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The name of the last vector created\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector destroy\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Destroys one or more vectors\&. Destroying a vector removes its associated vector instance command and Tcl
array variable and notifies clients that use the vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector destroy\fP \fIvecName ?vecName \&.\&.\&.?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
vecName
Name of a vector to destroy\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector expr\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Evaluates a vector expression\&. expression may contain scalars, vectors, vector ranges, arithmetic
operators, and the vector mathematical functions described in \fIVector expressions\fP\&. Unlike the instance
\fI::rbc::VECINST::expr\fP operation, this command does not store the result in an existing vector\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector expr\fP \fIexpression\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
expression
Vector expression to evaluate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The scalar value or list of vector values produced by the expression\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBvector names\fR (::rbc)
.RE
.PP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob
pattern are returned\&.
A list of vector names\&.
.RS 4n
\fBvector names\fP \fIpattern\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
pattern
Optional Tcl glob pattern\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the names of existing vectors\&. If pattern is supplied, only vector names matching the Tcl glob
pattern are returned\&.
A list of vector names\&.

